    "content": "You are a Tier-1 SOC Analyst. Output JSON only: {\"investigation_notes\": {\"Alert ID\": \"Unique identifier assigned to the alert within the SOC system.\", \"Start Time\": \"Timestamp indicating when the alert was generated.\", \"Alert Name\": \"Name or type of the alert as reported by the SIEM (e.g., Suspicious PowerShell Command Execution).\", \"Source IP\": \"IP address of the originator or user device associated with the alert.\", \"Destination IP\": \"Target IP address contacted or affected during the activity.\", \"Endpoint\": \"Hostname or device name where the alert originated.\", \"User\": \"Username linked to the activity or login session.\", \"Playbook Used\": \"Name of the investigation playbook automatically selected for analysis.\", \"Description\": \"Brief summary of what triggered the alert and the key observations identified during the investigation.\"}, \"classification\": \"LP (Low Priority) or HP (High Priority). Only write LP or HP for this part and no need for full form.\"}\n\nNote: Use the template above as a guide. If a specific field within the template guide does not exist or cannot be found in the alert evidence, do not include it in the final output. You may include up to 4-5 additional relevant keys inside investigation_notes, but only if they are truly needed, make sense, and would be useful for solving the ticket in a system like JIRA. Keep all fields single-level (no nested objects inside extra fields). All keys inside investigation_notes must be written in clear, regular English with spaces (e.g., 'IP Reputation Score', not snake_case or underscored keys). The Description should summarize what happened and the key observations clearly in full sentences, written as if a Tier 1 SOC analyst is reporting it, with a natural human-written tone. It should be between 2 and 4 brief sentences that are not too long, covering all main information. In addition to summarizing, the Description should provide reasoning for why the alert is considered Low Priority (LP) or High Priority (HP), without explicitly mentioning playbooks or SOPs. There is no need to include remediation actions in the Description. Output the final JSON in a readable, beautified format with proper indentation, not as a single-line minified string."
  },
  {
    "role": "user",
    "content": "Playbook: {\"playbook_name\": \"Suspicious PowerShell Triage\", \"description\": \"LLM-based SOP for triaging PowerShell alerts. Prioritizes identifying administrative tools via internal script decoding, file path validation (if available), dual-layer process lineage, and VirusTotal reputation.\", \"steps\": [{\"step_id\": 1, \"task\": \"Extract Execution Metadata & Lineage\", \"action\": \"Parse alert JSON for 'user', 'endpoint', 'scriptblock', 'file_path', 'initiating_process', 'parent_process', and 'process_sha256'.\", \"logic\": \"Metadata provides initial context. Note if 'file_path' is present; its absence may indicate a direct-to-memory execution (higher risk) or a logging limitation.\"}, {\"step_id\": 2, \"task\": \"External Reputation Check\", \"action\": \"Invoke 'check_virustotal' using the 'process_sha256' of the initiating process.\", \"tool_integration\": \"check_virustotal\", \"logic\": \"Determines if the binary responsible for spawning PowerShell is known-malicious. If detections >= 5, immediately flag for HP.\"}, {\"step_id\": 3, \"task\": \"Script Normalization and Content Analysis\", \"action\": \"Internally decode 'scriptblock' if Base64 encoded. Analyze for 'Admin Tool Signatures' (e.g., installer paths, authenticode checks) and cross-reference with the 'file_path' ONLY if it exists.\", \"logic\": \"Matches script intent (e.g., updater logic) with its physical location if known. If the script references a specific path that is NOT present in 'file_path', highlight the discrepancy.\"}, {\"step_id\": 4, \"task\": \"Correlate with Historical Activity\", \"action\": \"MAKE SURE TO INVOKE 'get_recent_events' and 'get_recent_similar_alerts' to understand context of the alert.\", \"tool_integration\": [\"get_recent_events\", \"get_recent_similar_alerts\"], \"logic\": \"Consistency in script logic and pathing across the environment typically suggests standard operational behavior (LP).\"}, {\"step_id\": 5, \"task\": \"External Network Context Validation (IP Address)\", \"action\": \"Extract external IP addresses from the decoded 'scriptblock', 'get_recent_events', and 'get_recent_similar_alerts' results, then invoke 'check_ip_reputation' (AbuseIPDB) for each identified IP. This is only if they exist in the script or correlated data. If not, skip this step.\", \"tool_integration\": \"check_ip_reputation\", \"logic\": \"IP reputation is used as contextual enrichment only. Regardless of AbuseIPDB score, the presence of hard-coded external IP communication within scripts exhibiting reverse shell, command-and-control, or interactive execution behavior should be treated as malicious by behavior. Reputation data is used to strengthen confidence, identify infrastructure reuse, and support campaign correlation\\u00e2\\u20ac\\u201dnot to downgrade severity.\"}, {\"step_id\": 6, \"task\": \"Final Triage Classification\", \"action\": \"Assign triage priority (LP or HP) by considering available context: process legitimacy, script behavior, execution environment, historical alerts, and network indicators if present.\", \"logic\": \"Classification rules:\\n\\n1. **Low Priority (LP)** if:\\n   - The user account executing the script is a known service or administrative account AND the script performs standard operational or maintenance tasks consistent with expected internal policies. \\n   - OR the script performs standard operational or maintenance tasks consistent with expected internal policies.\\n   - OR historical alerts show the same or similar scripts have executed previously without incident.\\n   \\n   > Note: LP does not require VirusTotal data, file paths, or IP reputation if they are unavailable. If those exist, they can optionally reinforce confidence.\\n\\n2. **High Priority (HP)** if:\\n   - The script shows unexpected or anomalous behavior outside normal administrative tasks.\\n   - OR the process is unknown, untrusted, or flagged as malicious (e.g., VirusTotal detections >=5).\\n   - OR command-and-control, reverse shell, or interactive execution behavior is observed.\\n   - OR the alert lacks context for critical operations and cannot be correlated with trusted service accounts or historical benign activity.\"}, {\"step_id\": 7, \"task\": \"Generate Investigation Summary\", \"action\": \"Construct a concise Description field including: 1) Script intent, 2) VT and AbuseIPDB results, if they exist, and 3) Final reasoning.\", \"logic\": \"Ensures the summary explicitly clarifies whether the triage was based on a physical file path or script behavior alone.\"}]}\nAlert: {\"alert_id\": \"SOC-4104\", \"alert_name\": \"Suspicious PowerShell Script Execution\", \"start_time\": \"2025-12-21T15:39:16.000Z\", \"source_ip\": \"10.0.0.1\", \"endpoint\": \"HOST-01\", \"user\": \"SYSTEM\", \"user_id\": \"S-1-5-18\", \"description\": \"A suspicious PowerShell script was executed on the host.\", \"evidence\": {\"scriptblock\": \"[Console]::OutputEncoding = [System.Text.Encoding]::UTF8; $ProgressPreference = 'SilentlyContinue'\\n\\n# Helper function to handle null values and escape special characters\\nfunction HandleString($value) {\\n    if ($null -eq $value) { \\n        return \\\"-\\\" \\n    } else { \\n        return $value -replace '\\\"', '\\\\\\\"'  # Escape double quotes\\n    }\\n}\\n\\ntry {\\n    $ouList = @()\\n    $domain = HandleString((Get-ADDomainController).Forest)\\n    $properties = @(\\\"Name\\\", \\\"ManagedBy\\\", \\\"Created\\\", \\\"Modified\\\", \\\"DistinguishedName\\\", \\\"ObjectGUID\\\", \\\"isCriticalSystemObject\\\")\\n\\n    $linkedOu = @{}\\n    foreach ($gpi in Get-ADOrganizationalUnit -Filter * | Get-GPInheritance) {\\n        if ($null -eq $gpi) {\\n            continue\\n        }\\n        $linkedGPO = @()\\n        foreach ($lgpo in $gpi.GpoLinks) {\\n            $linkedGPO += @{\\n                \\\"name\\\" = HandleString($lgpo.DisplayName)\\n                \\\"enabled\\\" = $lgpo.Enabled\\n                \\\"enforced\\\" = $lgpo.Enforced\\n            }\\n        }\\n        try {\\n            foreach ($lgpo in $gpi.InheritedGpoLinks) {\\n                $linkedGPO += @{\\n                    \\\"name\\\" = HandleString($lgpo.DisplayName)\\n                    \\\"enabled\\\" = $lgpo.Enabled\\n                    \\\"enforced\\\" = $lgpo.Enforced\\n                }\\n            }\\n        }\\n        catch {\\n        }\\n        $linkedOu[$gpi.name] = $linkedGPO\\n    }\\n\\n    foreach ($o in Get-ADOrganizationalUnit -Filter \\\"*\\\" -Properties $properties) {\\n        if ($null -eq $o) {\\n            continue\\n        }\\n        if ($o.DistinguishedName -notlike '*OU=*') {\\n            continue\\n        }\\n        $empty = $false\\n        if (-not (Get-ADObject -SearchBase $o -SearchScope OneLevel -Filter *)) {\\n            $empty = $true\\n        }\\n\\n        $ouIsCritical = if ($null -ne $o.isCriticalSystemObject) { $o.isCriticalSystemObject } else { $false }\\n\\n        $linkedComputers = @()\\n        foreach ($computer in Get-ADComputer -SearchBase $o.DistinguishedName -Filter *) {\\n            $linkedComputers += HandleString($computer.Name)\\n        }\\n\\n        $linkedGroups = @()\\n        foreach ($group in Get-ADGroup -SearchBase $o.DistinguishedName -Filter *) {\\n            $linkedGroups += HandleString($group.Name)\\n        }\\n\\n        $linkedUsers = @()\\n        foreach ($user in Get-ADUser -SearchBase $o.DistinguishedName -Filter *) {\\n            $linkedUsers += HandleString($user.Name)\\n        }\\n\\n        $ouList += @{\\n            \\\"domain\\\" = $domain\\n            \\\"ou\\\" = HandleString($o.Name)\\n            \\\"name\\\" = HandleString($o.Name)\\n            \\\"managedBy\\\" = HandleString($o.ManagedBy)\\n            \\\"modifiedwhen\\\" = $o.Modified.ToString(\\\"dd-MM-yyyy HH:mm:ss\\\")\\n            \\\"createdwhen\\\" = $o.Created.ToString(\\\"dd-MM-yyyy HH:mm:ss\\\")\\n            \\\"distinguishedName\\\" = HandleString($o.DistinguishedName)\\n            \\\"objectguid\\\" = HandleString($o.ObjectGUID)\\n            \\\"empty\\\" = $empty\\n            \\\"gplink\\\" = $linkedOu[$o.Name]\\n            \\\"iscriticalsystemobject\\\" = $ouIsCritical\\n            \\\"linkedComputers\\\" = $linkedComputers\\n            \\\"linkedGroups\\\" = $linkedGroups\\n            \\\"linkedUsers\\\" = $linkedUsers\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    $jsonResult = $ouList | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 5\\n\\n    # Define primary and fallback paths\\n    $primaryPath = \\\"C:\\\\Program Files (x86)\\\\CyberCNSAgent\\\\agent_temp\\\\ad_ous.json\\\"\\n    $fallbackPath = \\\"C:\\\\Windows\\\\cybercnsagent\\\\agent_temp\\\\ad_ous.json\\\"\\n\\n    # Get directory paths\\n    $primaryDir = Split-Path $primaryPath -Parent\\n    $fallbackDir = Split-Path $fallbackPath -Parent\\n\\n    # Determine final path\\n    if (Test-Path $primaryDir) {\\n        $finalPath = $primaryPath\\n    } else {\\n        # Ensure fallback directory exists\\n        if (-not (Test-Path $fallbackDir)) {\\n            New-Item -Path $fallbackDir -ItemType Directory -Force | Out-Null\\n        }\\n        $finalPath = $fallbackPath\\n    }\\n\\n    # Ensure directory exists before writing\\n    $finalDir = Split-Path $finalPath -Parent\\n    if (-not (Test-Path $finalDir)) {\\n        New-Item -Path $finalDir -ItemType Directory -Force | Out-Null\\n    }\\n\\n    # Save JSON result\\n    $jsonResult | Out-File -FilePath $finalPath -Encoding UTF8\\n\\n    Write-Output $jsonResult\\n}\\ncatch {\\n    ConvertTo-Json -Depth 5 -Compress @{\\n        status = $false\\n        msg = $( $_.Exception.Message )\\n        stackTrace = $_ | Out-String\\n    }\\n}\", \"os\": \"Windows Server 2022 Datacenter\"}, \"technique\": {\"name\": \"System Owner/User Discovery\", \"killchain_stage\": \"Discovery\"}}"
  }
]

